:"With the chain's other inputs fixed, a Higgs mass below 72.5 GeV gives a first-order transition.","label":"IMPORTED"},{"text":"Standard Model lattice value: 159.5 +- 1.5 GeV (D'Onofrio, Rummukainen 2016). The comparison is not blind.","label":"IMPORTED"},{"text":"The same pipeline with Standard Model inputs puts the maximum at 159.04 GeV; the published value at equal lattice spacing is 158.94 GeV.","label":"MEASURED"},{"text":"The chain's couplings are declared forms that fit. Their derivation is open.","label":"DECLARED"},{"text":"Source as built with one doublet: crossover at 145.1 GeV if its quartic and top couplings equal the declared row's, at 149.3 GeV if its Higgs and top masses do. These masses are not derived.","label":"DECLARED"},{"text":"The transition lies 9.0e-12 s after the start of the radiation era, 1.31e+32 ticks.","label":"IMPORTED"},{"text":"The reduction to three dimensions is perturbative, of relative order g^4, with an accuracy of about one percent.","label":"IMPORTED"}],"references":[{"cite":"Kajantie, Laine, Rummukainen, Shaposhnikov, Nucl. Phys. B458 (1996) 90: dimensional reduction","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-ph/9508379"},{"cite":"Kajantie, Laine, Rummukainen, Shaposhnikov, Nucl. Phys. B466 (1996) 189: lattice study of the transition","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-lat/9510020"},{"cite":"Laine, Nucl. Phys. B451 (1995) 484: exact relation of lattice and continuum parameters","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-lat/9504001"},{"cite":"Laine, Rajantie, Nucl. Phys. B513 (1998) 471: lattice-continuum relations","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-lat/9705003"},{"cite":"Rummukainen, Tsypin, Kajantie, Laine, Shaposhnikov, Nucl. Phys. B532 (1998) 283: the endpoint","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-lat/9805013"},{"cite":"D'Onofrio, Rummukainen, Phys. Rev. D93 (2016) 025003: the Standard Model crossover on the lattice","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1508.07161"},{"cite":"D'Onofrio, Rummukainen, Tranberg, Phys. Rev. Lett. 113 (2014) 141602","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1404.3565"}]}
